    "reason": "At p=1% the noise delayed 5 s 4 responses in 4 of the run's 299 sessions (session 3: the settle of call 2; session 10: the fail of call 0; session 74: the fail of call 3; and 1 more session). A delayed response reaches the engine 5 s after the caller sent it, so for 5 s the engine still has the call in flight while the caller's log shows it closed. Session 21 lost no response, yet it is explained by none of fixed/non-reflexive/no marks/atomic, fixed/non-reflexive/request/atomic, fixed/non-reflexive/error/atomic, fixed/non-reflexive/error+request/atomic, and its verdicts differ from the noiseless run's session 21: the engine draws its recording schedule from one stream for all its sessions, and the calls left open by the lost responses took extra draws from it, so every later session got another draw of the same non-atomic schedule; in session 21 (4 calls) that draw recorded a call after its admission, which no atomic profile does. The noise did not invent this behaviour: the engine is non-atomic, and the noise moved where it shows. The run reports 4 profiles where the noiseless run reported 8. Every profile it reports is still in the true profile's class, the true profile among them, and no session is left unexplained (residual 0, nothing 'not refuted'), so the run has nothing to announce: the change narrows the report, it does not mislead it.",
